# Criticism

## The problem (problem)
For each of this prototype's own checks, name a rewrite of an input under which the check's verdict ought to move. Propose a kernel, a transform, and an input. The executor will run it; the catalogue says which pairs anyone has written down already. A pair that moves and is not catalogued is a boundary nobody has recorded; a pair the catalogue says moves and that does not move is a defect in the catalogue.

## Proposals so far (proposals)
[2e242386c0e45309] (mini.proposal.v1)
What the verdicts establish, and the one variable left untried.

Three proposals wrote seven triples, but the verdicts record one executed pair per proposal — the single triple each proposal carried in its commitments field; the rest were prose. All three executed pairs were unrunnable. The executed set:

- Pair 1: kernel stated as a full descriptive sentence ("equals-'hello' — accept iff the input is exactly the characters h,e,l,l,o (byte-strict exact match)"), transform stated as prose with the expected motion and a conditional report demand embedded in the string, input stated with literal quote marks ("' hello '").
- Pair 2: everything bare — "json-number", "append '0'", "1e".
- Pair 3: kernel descriptive, transform and input bare — "append 'x'", "hello".

Uniform unrunnability across that set is the diagnostic fact. The kernels differ (two shapes), the string forms differ (descriptive vs bare), the inputs differ; nothing is common to all three except the pipeline itself. Two hypotheses survive: (a) the executor ran nothing this cycle; (b) the executor is up but neither kernel naming resolved to a check the prototype contains. The input-format hypothesis is disfavoured alone — two of three inputs are bare alphanumeric strings. The transform hypothesis is weakened — trim and append are the most canonical rewrites — but Pair 1's transform field carried prose no identifier registry would match, so it is not eliminated.

The untried cell. For the exact-match kernel, only the descriptive string form has been executed (Pairs 1 and 3). For the json-number kernel, only the bare form has been executed (Pair 2). So the bare form of the exact-match kernel is the one cell of the form-by-kernel grid no run has touched — and it can be reached while changing nothing else, because Pair 3's transform and input were already bare.

Triple 8. Kernel: equals-'hello' (bare identifier; no gloss, no em dash, no expected-motion text riding in the string). Transform: append 'x' (unchanged from the rejected triple). Input: hello (unchanged). Expected motion: accept → reject.

The kernel-level diagnostic is unchanged from its earlier statement: the kernel is byte-strict, 'hellox' is the accepted string with exactly one character added, so motion confirms the exact-match reading, and stillness refutes it in favour of the laxer readings, since starts-with-'hello' and contains-'hello' both accept 'hellox'. What this run adds is the test of string form itself.

What each outcome settles. Runs and moves (accept → reject): the descriptive string was the blocker; the record gains its first executed pair, and catalogue classification — recorded boundary or unrecorded one — becomes meaningful for the first time. Runs and stands still: the kernel is laxer than exact-match, or the transform did not append; the report should say which, since a stillness here is a finding about the kernel's reading, not a pipeline fault. Unrunnable again: both forms have then failed for one kernel (descriptive in Pairs 1 and 3, bare here) and the bare form failed for the other (Pair 2); no third guess from outside can separate "executor down" from "wrong identifiers", because the prototype's check list is visible only to the executor. So the unrunnable verdict must carry a reason code: executor-unavailable; or kernel-identifier-not-found, together with the list of the prototype's actual kernel identifiers; or transform-identifier-not-found, with that list; or input-unparseable. With the lists, the next proposal aims at real names; without them, every future proposal is a blind guess and every unrunnable verdict is a dead end instead of a map.

One flag on the catalogue flags. Every verdict so far says "not catalogued", but the only catalogue block visible in this cycle is cut before any pair list appears. If the lookup ran against the full catalogue text, "not catalogued" is evidence; if it ran against the same truncated text I see, it is a default and says nothing about novelty. The report should state which text the lookup ran against — one line, and it decides whether every "not catalogued" in the record is usable.

Standing limitations, unchanged. The catalogue's pair list and move flags remain beyond the cut, so I claim novelty for nothing and nominate no defect case; classification is the run's job against the full text. The correction pair from the previous proposal (json-number, prepend '-', input '-1', expected accept → reject) stays queued behind the identifier question — it is worth running only once some identifier form has resolved.

Priority if only one triple is run: this one. It is the smallest change that separates the two surviving hypotheses, and it is the only proposal on the table whose failure mode itself produces information.
commitments: {"kernel": "equals-'hello'", "transform": "append 'x'", "input": "hello"}
[d0f8332533e2215f] (mini.proposal.v1)
The untested variable is the transform string's form, and it has been suspect in every executed run.

Three pairs have been executed, all unrunnable. The last proposal's form-grid (kernel-string form against kernel) left one dimension untouched, and re-reading the executed set, every run sits in its suspect region:

- Pair 1's transform was prose with an embedded demand — unmatchable under any identifier registry.
- Pair 2's transform was "append '0'" — quoted-argument syntax.
- Pair 3's transform was "append 'x'" — the same quoted-argument syntax.

No executed pair has ever carried a bare, argument-free transform identifier. If the registry holds bare names — "trim", "lowercase", fixed argument-free rewrites, with arguments passed some other way or not at all — then all three pairs fail at the transform stage, and the uniform unrunnability is explained with no hypothesis about the executor being down or the kernel names being wrong. That is now the strongest single-factor explanation on the table, and the pending Triple 8 does not test it: Triple 8's transform is "append 'x'", the same quoted-arg form whose matchability is in question.

Triple 9. Kernel: equals-'hello' (bare identifier, character-for-character the same string Triple 8 commits). Transform: lowercase (bare, argument-free — no quotes, no argument, no prose). Input: HELLO. Expected motion: reject → accept.

Design. The pair holds the kernel string identical to the pending Triple 8 and varies the transform form and the input, so the two pending pairs read as one experiment: same kernel, two transform forms. Lowercase is chosen over trim deliberately: trim's natural input " hello " risks an executor that strips the input field itself, which would defeat the transform silently and masquerade as kernel stillness; "HELLO" is an unambiguous bare token and lowercase on it is unambiguous. The pair also executes, in canonical string form, the content of the never-run Triple 2 of ac1042606b813c15 (lowercase on 'HELLO'), whose carrier proposal's commitments went to Triple 1 instead.

Why the motion is expected. The exact-match kernel rejects "HELLO" — case differs; lowercase yields "hello", which it accepts. The pair also moves under the starts-with and case-sensitive contains readings, so motion here does not by itself separate exact-match from the laxer readings; that work stays with Triple 8's append-'x' probe, where it belongs. This pair's job is the form test, and any runnable verdict — moving or still — settles it.

What the outcomes settle, jointly with the pending Triple 8.

- Both run: bare identifiers resolve end to end, and the motions carry the kernel diagnostics — both moving confirms byte-strict exact-match on two independent rewrites; mine moving while Triple 8 stands still leaves starts-with and contains live, and the prepend probe already named ('xhello': contains-yes, starts-with-no) separates them; mine standing still means the kernel already accepts "HELLO" (a case-insensitive reading) or the transform did not lowercase, and the report should say which.
- Mine runs, Triple 8 unrunnable: the executor is up and the kernel string resolves, so Triple 8's failure lands on its transform — the quoted-argument syntax is unmatchable while bare names resolve. That one finding explains all three historical unrunnables, and the queued pairs should be re-issued in whatever form the transform list actually names.
- Mine unrunnable, Triple 8 runs: "lowercase" is absent from the transform registry while "append 'x'" is present; the verdict should carry the actual transform identifier list so the next proposal aims at real names.
- Both unrunnable: two different transform forms failed on the same kernel string, so the transform field is exonerated as the sole blocker and the suspects narrow to the kernel identifier itself or a down executor. Then the reason-code demand of 2e242386c0e45309 becomes the only way forward, and a second round of bare "unrunnable" verdicts with no reason codes, issued after that demand, is itself a finding: the verdict channel does not carry reasons, and every further proposal is a blind guess by construction.

A structural point either way. The breaking-direction pairs named for the json-number kernel (append 'e' on '1', prepend '-' on '-1') all need argument-bearing transforms. If the registry turns out to be argument-free only, those pairs are unexpressible as stated and the record can carry only argument-free rewrites — a constraint on the whole boundary map, worth knowing now rather than after five more unrunnable rounds.

Catalogue flags, unchanged and still unanswered. The only catalogue block visible to me is cut before its pair list — its note reads "Kernel and transform pairs already written down, and whether the kernel's verdict is known to move under th…" — so I claim novelty for nothing and nominate no defect case; classification is the run's job against the full text. And every verdict so far says "not catalogued"; that is usable evidence only if the lookup ran against the full catalogue text rather than the truncated block I see. One line in the report settles it.

Priority. Triple 8 keeps the priority it claimed; this pair is its designed complement and should run beside it. If only one executes this cycle, Triple 8 goes first and this pair follows — the two together are the minimal set that separates the three surviving single-factor explanations: executor down, kernel identifier wrong, transform form wrong.
